# RustDesk Console - manual machine names and OS overrides
#
# Format:  <rustdesk id> = <name>              a machine name
#          os:<rustdesk id> = <os string>      an OS label
#
# Names are normally discovered automatically: the collector asks each peer's IP
# for its NetBIOS workstation name, falling back to a reverse-DNS lookup.
# That fails in one case you cannot fix on the network - a peer whose traffic
# arrives NAT'd shows up as the gateway address, which is not the machine, so
# the lookup either times out or returns the router. Name those peers here.
#
# An entry below overrides whatever was discovered. Edits apply within ~30s;
# no restart needed. Keyed on the RustDesk ID because that is stable, while the
# peer's IP is not.
#
# OS is normally reported by the machine itself (deployed workstations) or
# guessed from its SMB build. The guess cannot tell 24H2 / 25H2 / 26H2 apart -
# they share build 26100 over the wire - so an unmanaged machine on a newer
# release may read low. Override it here; an os: line wins over both.
#
# Example:
# 100000001 = WORKSTATION-01
# os:100000001 = Windows 11 26H2
